      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Escrow, in the context of buying a house, is a trust account that holds funds until certain conditions are met. It's a way to ensure that the transaction unfolds smoothly, much like a team of skilled, yet mischievous, goblins working together to build a intricate trap. You see, in the world of real estate, there are often multiple parties involved, and escrow acts as a neutral third party to hold funds until all parties agree on the terms.\n\nThink of it like a group of goblins, each representing a different stakeholder (e.g., the seller, lender, and you). They all need to agree on the terms of the sale before the funds are released. If one goblin is being particularly stubborn, the others can hold the funds hostage until they come to an agreement. This way, everyone's interests are protected, and the transaction can proceed without a hitch.\n\nIn Toronto, where the housing market can be as unpredictable as a goblin's mood swings, having an escrow account in place can provide an added layer of security. It's a safeguard against any unexpected issues that might arise during the closing process. So, to answer your question, you need an escrow account to ensure that the funds are",
