      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy. Let me break it down for you in a more straightforward way.\n\nIn an escrow account, a third-party escrow agent holds the funds until the conditions of the sale are met. The agent is usually a bank or a specialized escrow company. When you set up an escrow account, you deposit the funds, which are then held in a separate account until the sale is finalized.\n\nHere's what happens with the money:\n\n* The escrow agent holds the funds until the seller completes any outstanding obligations, such as:\n\t+ Repaying any outstanding mortgage or other debts\n\t+ Paying any taxes or fees\n\t+ Providing any necessary documentation\n* Once the seller has fulfilled their obligations, the escrow agent releases the funds to the buyer.\n* If there's a dispute between the buyer and seller, the escrow agent can hold the funds until the issue is resolved.\n\nIf there's a dispute, the escrow agent will typically follow these steps:\n\n* The buyer and seller will present their case to the escrow agent, who will review the evidence and make a decision.\n* If the dispute is related to the sale itself, the escrow agent may need to involve",
